 <div class="c-article-equation" id="Equ29"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">$$\begin{aligned} \dot{\sigma }^{2}_{2}+3H\sigma ^{2}_{2}=\dot{\sigma }^{3}_{3}+3H\sigma ^{3}_{3} =-\frac{1}{3Y^{2}}. \end{aligned}$$</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (29)
                </div></div>

 <div class="c-article-equation" id="Equ30"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">$$\begin{aligned} \frac{d(\sigma ^{2})}{dt}+6H\sigma ^{2}=\frac{2}{3Y^{2}}\Bigl (\frac{\dot{X}}{X} -\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr ). \end{aligned}$$</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (30)
                </div></div>
NEIGHBOR RAW: <p>Consequently, the rate of the shear scalar <span class="mathjax-tex">\(d(\sigma ^{2})/dt=\dot{\sigma }^{\mu }_\nu \sigma ^\nu _\mu \)</span> satisfies</p>
NEIGHBOR RAW: <p>The right-hand side in this equation is positive, which indicates that the shear scalar grows with decreasing <i>a</i> faster than <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\sim a^{-6}\)</span>.</p>

 <div class="c-article-equation" id="Equ31"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">$$\begin{aligned} \frac{d\theta }{ds}=-\frac{1}{3}\theta ^{2}-2(\sigma ^{2}-\omega ^{2}) +w^{\mu }{}_{;\mu }-R_{\mu \nu }u^{\mu } u^\nu , \end{aligned}$$</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (31)
                </div></div>

 <div class="c-article-equation" id="Equ32"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">$$\begin{aligned} 2\kappa \alpha n_{\text {f}}^{2}&gt;2\sigma ^{2}+\kappa \epsilon . \end{aligned}$$</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (32)
                </div></div>

 <div class="c-article-equation" id="Equ33"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">$$\begin{aligned} \frac{1}{\sqrt{-g}}\frac{d(\sqrt{-g}n_{\text {f}})}{dt}=\beta H^{4}, \end{aligned}$$</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (33)
                </div></div>

 <div class="c-article-equation" id="Equ34"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">$$\begin{aligned} \dot{n}_{\text {f}}+3Hn_{\text {f}}=\beta H^{4},\quad \frac{d(n^{2}_{\text {f}})}{dt} +6Hn^{2}_{\text {f}}=2\beta n_{\text {f}}H^{4}. \end{aligned}$$</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (34)
                </div></div>

 <div class="c-article-equation" id="Equ35"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">$$\begin{aligned} \frac{2\beta n_{\text {f}}}{81}\Bigl (\frac{\dot{X}}{X} +\frac{2\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr )^{4}&gt;\frac{2}{3Y^{2}}\Bigl (\frac{\dot{X}}{X} -\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr ). \end{aligned}$$</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (35)
                </div></div>
NEIGHBOR RAW: <p>To avoid a singularity, the rate of <span class="mathjax-tex">\(n^{2}_{\text {f}}\)</span> must exceed the rate of <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\sigma ^{2}\)</span>. Comparing (<a data-track="click" data-track-action="equation anchor" data-track-label="link" href="/article/10.1007/s10714-021-02790-7#Equ34">34</a>) with (<a data-track="click" data-track-action="equation anchor" data-track-label="link" href="/article/10.1007/s10714-021-02790-7#Equ30">30</a>) gives</p>
NEIGHBOR RAW: <p>After the formation of the event horizon, at the instant when (<a data-track="click" data-track-action="equation anchor" data-track-label="link" href="/article/10.1007/s10714-021-02790-7#Equ32">32</a>) is reached, this inequality must be satisfied to ensure that (<a data-track="click" data-track-action="equation anchor" data-track-label="link" href="/article/10.1007/s10714-021-02790-7#Equ32">32</a>) continues to hold.</p>
